  "title": "Insurance and Reinsurance",
  "question": "Who promises protection, controls the supporting assets, transfers or retains risk, and ultimately pays or recoups a covered loss?",
  "bluf": "Insurance links contractual access to risk transfer and assets supporting long-dated promises. Named policies, treaties, collateral, public programs and claim records determine who owes what; government loss sharing is case-specific rather than inevitable. The September Japan comparison finds real AIR convergence with the U.S./Bermuda machine, but not identity: Japan retains a national supervisor, newly live ESR regime, JGB-heavy life balance sheets, major mutuals and an explicit government earthquake-reinsurance tail, while current AIR stock is concentrated in foreign-owned and listed carriers.",
